STEP 5 — THE PORT'S REFUSAL NOW REACHES THE FAILURE MESSAGE (DoD 1, second clause: never a bare "did not prove all checks"). Two files.

(a) apps/daemon/src/goals/goal-scoped-close-test-fixtures.ts — INJECT A RECORDING EXECUTOR.
Built `createCriterionCheckExecutor()` explicitly and wrapped it in a plain object (NOT `vi.spyOn`; this module is a fixture, not a test file) that keeps the last non-null `refusal` and forwards `close()` to the inner port:
  const port = createCriterionCheckExecutor();
  const recorder: { refusal: CriterionCheckExecutionResult["refusal"] } = { refusal: null };
  const executor: CriterionCheckExecutor = {
    async run(input, onStarted) {
      const result = await port.run(input, onStarted);
      if (result.refusal !== null) recorder.refusal = result.refusal;
      return result;
    },
    close: () => port.close(),
  };
passed through as `createCriterionEvidenceService({ …, executor })`. The seam already existed — criterion-service.ts:19 forwards `executor` to criterion-runner.ts:27,38 — so no production signature changed. No manifest edit owed: `@moe/runner` is already a `workspace:*` dependency of `@moe/daemon` and criterion-runner.ts:2 already imports from the bare specifier; `pnpm typecheck` EXIT 0 with `apps/daemon typecheck: Done` is the compiled proof that the bare import resolves from this package.

The win32 throw now appends the reason when there is one:
  const { refusal } = recorder;
  throw new Error(`scoped criterion execution did not prove all checks${refusal === null
    ? "" : `; execution port refused ${refusal.code} at layer ${refusal.layer}`}`);
Destructuring into a local before the ternary is deliberate: `recorder.refusal` is only ever assigned inside a closure, and reading it fresh keeps the declared union instead of relying on TS narrowing across a call boundary. On this host the message would have read "…did not prove all checks; execution port refused PROCESS_BOUNDARY_BROKER_REFUSED at layer BROKER_PROTOCOL" — the exact sentence whose absence sent three earlier sessions after three wrong causes.

CLOSE IS FORWARDED, AND THAT IS EPIC RAIL 4, NOT A DETAIL. criterion-runner.ts:132 (`async close() { closed = true; await executor.close(); … }`) is the ONLY thing that closes the port; a wrapper that dropped `close()` would leak a broker process on every scoped-close test. It is reached on the failure path too: the fixture's `catch` at the end calls `cleanup()` -> `service.close()` -> `runner.close()` -> `port.close()`. Construction cannot leak either — `createCriterionCheckExecutor()` only allocates a Set and spawns nothing until `run`.

(b) apps/daemon/src/criterion-evidence/criterion-integrated.test.ts — ASSERT THE PORT DID NOT REFUSE, inside the win32 branch ONLY (the non-win32 branch legitimately expects `PROCESS_BOUNDARY_PLATFORM_UNSUPPORTED` and is untouched). Placed BEFORE the existing `toMatchObject` so the refusal codes appear verbatim in the failure output rather than as an opaque status mismatch:
  expect(execution.mock.results).toHaveLength(2);
  const refusals = (await Promise.all(execution.mock.results.map(async (entry) =>
    entry.type === "return" ? (await entry.value).refusal : null))).filter((row) => row !== null);
  expect(refusals, "criterion execution port refused; scoped checks cannot complete").toEqual([]);

THE LENGTH ASSERTION IS LOAD-BEARING, not decoration. Project rail: a swept case must assert the case was actually generated. With an empty `mock.results` the refusal sweep would collect nothing and pass while proving nothing — the sweep would be vacuous exactly when the port was never reached. `toHaveLength(2)` pins the two approved criteria (crit-api, crit-ui) so the sweep has real cases to filter. The `entry.type === "return"` guard keeps a thrown call from being read as a refusal-free result.

NOTHING ELSE MOVED: no passing expectation changed, no byteCount changed (still 16/16), no non-win32 branch touched, no `it.skip`, no `runIf`. Existing spy at :32 reused.

